{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/pulls/434","id":3859511177,"node_id":"PR_kwDOSbOoU87mC3eJ","html_url":"https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/434","diff_url":"https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/434.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/434.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/434","number":434,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Generalize solver recognizers from memoized lookup tables to compositional rules (#433)","user":{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Closes #433. Follow-up to #423 (now merged via #424 into `main`).\n\n## What & why\n\nIssue #433 asks the solver's recognizers to move from **memoized lookup tables**\n(extend a list to support one more case) toward **compositional rules** (derive\nthe answer from structure, so unseen-but-well-formed inputs work without editing\na table). It sets three acceptance criteria, all delivered here.\n\n### AC1 — Audit of every handler recognizer\n\n`docs/case-studies/issue-433/README.md` classifies all ~46\n`SPECIALIZED_HANDLERS` recognizers as **FIXED ENUMERATION**, **COMPOSITIONAL**,\nor **HYBRID**, with the recognizer location and the concrete evidence (constant\n/ keyword list / structural basis) for each. It tallies ~29 already-compositional\nhandlers, 4 hybrids, and ~10 fixed-enumeration generalization candidates, ranks\nthe worst offenders (`opinion_question`, `number_riddle`, `algorithm`,\n`source_refresh`/`source_conflict`/`network_query`), and lists compositional\nexemplars worth imitating.\n\n### AC2 — Installation-conversion command recognizer generalized\n\n`installation_conversion::looks_like_command` no longer matches a fixed\n`PREFIXES` tool whitelist. It now reasons about command **shape** and\n**provenance**:\n\n- `is_executable_head` — first token must look like an executable/path\n  (lowercase/digit/`./` lead), so *any* tool passes on shape (`bun`, `deno`,\n  `uv`, `just`, `zig`, `pdm`, `poetry`, `nix`, …) while capitalized/non-ASCII\n  prose heads are rejected.\n- shell composition (`|`, `&&`, `||`, `;`) is accepted unconditionally.\n- an English function-word check rejects wrapped prose with a command-like head.\n- `Provenance::{CodeSpan, BareLine}` — fenced/inline code is trusted by shape;\n  a raw document line must prove itself (needs an argument or path; a bare line\n  embedding a code span is treated as prose because the inline collector already\n  lifted the real command out).\n\n`describe_command` was likewise generalized from a fixed substring table to\nverb/object intent inference (`ParsedCommand` + `classify_verb`), keyed on the\nverb (`install`/`build`/`test`/…) not the tool, with a synthesized fallback for\nunknown verbs.\n\n**No regression + new false-positive coverage:**\n- the #423 top-100-repo corpus stays green;\n- `unlisted_tools_still_route_through_install_conversion` (bun/deno/uv/zig);\n- `prose_bullets_do_not_leak_into_generated_scripts` (engine level);\n- 8 private-function recognizer tests in the source-mirror suite;\n- the browser worker (`src/web/formal_ai_worker.js`) was mirrored and the\n  `experiments/issue-423-js-installation-conversion.mjs` harness asserts the same\n  unlisted-tool and prose-rejection behavior for cross-runtime parity.\n\n### AC3 — A coding handler reconstructed from the meta-algorithm primitives\n\nThe case study reconstructs the independently-written `numeric_list` (issue\n#395) coding handler purely as an instantiation of the seven\n`ALGORITHM_CONSTRUCTION_STAGES` meta-algorithm primitives\n(`collect_corpus → … → promote_capability`), mapping each stage to its\nalready-existing `numeric_list` counterpart. Because the two handlers were built\nfor unrelated problem classes yet decompose into the same primitives, the\nmeta-algorithm is shown to be a general coding-handler construction recipe; the\ndoc closes with a concrete stage-by-stage migration recipe for the\n`algorithm` fixed-enumeration handler.\n\n## Tests\n\n```\ncargo fmt -- --check\ncargo clippy --all-targets --all-features      # clean\ncargo test --test unit                         # 840 passed\ncargo test --test source                        # 397 passed\ncargo test --test integration                   # 35 passed\nnode --check src/web/formal_ai_worker.js\nnode experiments/issue-423-js-installation-conversion.mjs   # all checks pass\n```\n\n## Notes\n\n- `main` is merged in; the #424 dependency is resolved (it landed in `main`).\n- A minor carve-out was added to the issue-103 prose lint so verbatim\n  third-party GitHub captures archived under `docs/case-studies/*/raw-data` are\n  not subject to the project's own deferred-label prose rule.\n","created_at":"2026-06-13T09:04:21Z","updated_at":"2026-06-13T11:14:59Z","closed_at":"2026-06-13T11:14:58Z","merged_at":"2026-06-13T11:14:58Z","merge_commit_sha":"3412d87404b54d88db2e90d69f815523d9de89ad","assignees":[{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://